\tag{frap}
\title{Faces, flags, and the symmetry of a shape}

\p{A local turn needs a shape-level answer to two questions: which faces are
incident, and which symmetries preserve those incidences? An abstract polytope
retains the incidence of vertices, edges and faces without prescribing
distances or angles. Schulte's flag formulation
\citet{Section 2}{schulte2017regular} leads from these incidences to
symmetries and to the extension of a facet symmetry used in
[local puzzle moves](frap-0009).}

\transclude{frap-000D}
\transclude{frap-000E}
\transclude{frap-000F}

\p{The triangle and tetrahedron calculation now supplies a concrete check:
the flag graph records which flags are adjacent; a vertex figure records
the local neighborhood seen from one vertex. With those objects in hand, the
next steps ask which incidence-preserving maps act on all flags and how a
symmetry of one facet can be extended.}

